    "# DS3040 – Lab Assignment 6: CNNs, BiLSTM, Feature Extraction & Visualization\n",
    "### Complete Solution with Deep Conceptual Explanations\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## 🧠 PART 0 — Conceptual Foundation (Read First)\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "### 1. What Is a CNN and Why This Architecture?\n",
    "\n",
    "**Intuition:** Images are NOT just a list of pixels. A cat's ear looks like a cat's ear whether it's in the top-left or bottom-right of an image. CNNs exploit this **spatial structure** using filters that slide across the image.\n",
    "\n",
    "**How it works:** A convolution filter is a small matrix (e.g., 3×3) that gets multiplied element-wise with each patch of the image, then summed. This detects local patterns (edges, curves, textures). Stacking layers detects increasingly complex patterns (edges → shapes → objects).\n",
    "\n",
    "**Architecture breakdown for this lab:**\n",
    "```\n",
    "Input (3×32×32)          ← CIFAR-10: RGB images, 32×32 pixels\n",
    "Conv2d(32 filters, 3×3)  ← 32 filters each detect a different low-level pattern\n",
    "ReLU                     ← Non-linearity (remove negatives)\n",
    "MaxPool2d(2×2)           ← Halve spatial size: 32→16, keep strongest activations\n",
    "Conv2d(64 filters, 3×3)  ← 64 higher-level pattern detectors\n",
    "ReLU\n",
    "MaxPool2d(2×2)           ← 16→8\n",
    "Flatten                  ← (64, 8, 8) → 4096\n",
    "Linear(128)              ← 4096 → 128 (THIS IS THE FEATURE VECTOR WE EXTRACT)\n",
    "ReLU\n",
    "Linear(10)               ← 128 → 10 class scores\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "**Technical depth:**\n",
    "- Conv2d with 32 filters, 3×3 kernel on RGB (3 channels): output has 32 channels, each is the convolution of all 3 input channels with a 3×3 kernel → `32 × (3 × 3 × 3 + 1) = 896` parameters.\n",
    "- MaxPooling is NOT learned. It just takes the max value in each 2×2 window.\n",
    "- `padding=1` keeps spatial dimensions the same after convolution: `(32-3+2*1)/1+1 = 32`. Without padding, each conv shrinks the map.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "### 2. Feature Extraction — What Does It Mean?\n",
    "\n",
    "**Intuition:** After training, a neural network is a feature extractor. The last few layers encode a compressed, semantically meaningful representation of the input. For an image of a 'cat', the 128-dim vector captures \"cat-like\" properties, independent of position, color variation, etc.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why extract from the FC layer (128-dim), not the raw image?**\n",
    "- Raw pixels: 3×32×32 = 3072 dims, no semantic structure\n",
    "- After conv layers: spatially organized but still image-specific\n",
    "- After FC(128): compact, class-discriminative — ideal for visualization\n",
    "\n",
    "**Implementation trick:** Register a hook on the FC layer to capture its output without modifying the model.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "### 3. PCA — Why and How\n",
    "\n",
    "**Intuition:** PCA finds the directions of maximum variance in high-dimensional data and projects onto those directions. It's like finding the \"most interesting\" axes.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Mathematically:**\n",
    "1. Center the data: `X_centered = X - mean(X)`\n",
    "2. Compute covariance matrix: `C = X_centered.T @ X_centered / (n-1)`\n",
    "3. Eigen-decompose: `C = V Λ V.T` where columns of V are principal components\n",
    "4. Project: `X_pca = X_centered @ V[:, :k]` for k components\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why use PCA BEFORE t-SNE?**\n",
    "- t-SNE is O(n²) in dimensionality — 128 or 256 dims makes it very slow\n",
    "- PCA first reduces to 50 dims (fast, linear) removing noise\n",
    "- t-SNE then operates on cleaner 50-dim data → faster + better results\n",
    "- **What if you skip PCA?** t-SNE on 128 dims still works but is 10-20× slower and may produce noisier results.\n",
    "\n",
    "**What if you DON'T standardize?** Features with large variance dominate PCA. Always StandardScaler before PCA.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "### 4. t-SNE — How It Differs from PCA\n",
    "\n",
    "| | PCA | t-SNE |\n",
    "|--|-----|-------|\n",
    "| Type | Linear | Non-linear |\n",
    "| Objective | Maximize variance | Preserve local neighborhoods |\n",
    "| Speed | Fast (O(nd²)) | Slow (O(n²)) |\n",
    "| Global structure | ✓ Preserved | ✗ NOT preserved |\n",
    "| Cluster separation | Moderate | Excellent |\n",
    "| Deterministic | ✓ | ✗ (stochastic) |\n",
    "| Use case | Preprocessing, exploration | Visualization only |\n",
    "\n",
    "**How t-SNE works:**\n",
    "1. In high-dim space: compute probability that point i is a 'neighbor' of j using Gaussian\n",
    "2. In 2D space: compute same probability using t-distribution (heavier tails)\n",
    "3. Minimize KL-divergence between these two distributions using gradient descent\n",
    "4. Result: nearby points in high-dim → nearby in 2D\n",
    "\n",
    "**Interpreting t-SNE:** Clusters = similar representations. Well-separated clusters = model learned discriminative features. Overlapping clusters = model confused between those classes.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "### 5. BiLSTM — What Makes It Different\n",
    "\n",
    "**Standard LSTM:** Reads left → right. At position 5, you know words 1-5 but not 6-10.\n",
    "\n",
    "**BiLSTM:** Runs TWO LSTMs:\n",
    "- Forward LSTM: reads left → right → captures \"what came before\"\n",
    "- Backward LSTM: reads right → left → captures \"what comes after\"\n",
    "\n",
    "```\n",
    "Sentence: \"The movie was not good\"\n",
    "\n",
    "Forward:  The → movie → was → not → good\n",
    "                                       ↑\n",
    "                              h_fwd (saw 'not' before 'good')\n",
    "\n",
    "Backward: good → not → was → movie → The\n",
    "                                       ↑\n",
    "                              h_bwd (also saw 'not' before 'good', from other side)\n",
    "\n",
    "Combined: [h_fwd | h_bwd] → richer representation\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "**Technical detail:** If hidden_dim=128, BiLSTM output is 256-dim (128 forward + 128 backward). We take the LAST hidden states: `h_n[-2]` (last forward layer) and `h_n[-1]` (last backward layer).\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "### 6. ResNet-18 — Why Better Than Basic CNN?\n",
    "\n",
    "**The problem with deep CNNs:** Adding more layers should help, but gradients vanish in very deep networks → deeper = worse (degradation problem).\n",
    "\n",
    "**ResNet's solution — Skip connections:**\n",
    "```\n",
    "Standard:   x → Conv → BN → ReLU → Conv → BN →      F(x)      → ReLU\n",
    "ResNet:     x → Conv → BN → ReLU → Conv → BN → F(x) + x       → ReLU\n",
    "                                                 ↑\n",
    "                                           identity shortcut\n",
    "```\n",
    "The network learns the **residual** F(x) = H(x) - x instead of H(x) directly.\n",
    "If the layer isn't useful, F(x) → 0 and the block becomes identity.\n",
    "Gradients flow directly through the skip connection → no vanishing gradient.\n",
    "\n",
    "**ResNet-18 architecture:** 18 weight layers: 1 conv + 8 residual blocks (2 conv each) + 1 FC.\n",
    "Pre-trained on ImageNet (1.2M images, 1000 classes) → already knows edges, textures, shapes.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Feature extraction from ResNet-18:** Remove the final FC(1000) layer. The 512-dim output of the avgpool layer is the feature vector.\n",
    "\n",
    "---"
